</image>
</sidebyside>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-ftli">
<title>Line Integrals in Conservative Fields</title>

<p>
Next, we will study the line integrals of conservative fields, in the
sense of <xref ref="def-conservative-concepts"/>. As you
will recognize, if you think of the gradient of a vector field as its
derivative, then the fundamental theorem of line integrals makes a
similar statement for vector-valued functions as the one made for
definite integrals of scalar-valued functions, i.e., the one that you
know as FTC II.<fn>The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part II: if
<m>f</m> is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m> and <m>F</m> is any antiderivative
of <m>f</m> on that interval, then
<m>\int_a^b f(x)\,dx = F(b) - F(a)</m>. It evaluates a definite integral
from the values of an antiderivative at the two endpoints, and
<xref ref="thm-ftli"/> does the same for a line integral, using the
values of a potential function at the two endpoints of the
curve.</fn>
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-ftli">
<title>Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>C</m> be a smooth curve joining the point <m>A</m> to the point
<m>B</m> in the plane or in space and parametrized by
<m>\mathbf r(t)</m>. Let <m>f</m> be a differentiable function with a
continuous gradient vector <m>\mathbf F = \nabla f</m> on a domain
<m>D</m> containing <m>C</m>. Then
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-ftli">
\int_C \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r = f(B) - f(A).
</men>
</statement>
<proof>
<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,11.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the proof of the fundamental theorem of line integrals.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</proof>
</theorem>

<p>
In <xref ref="fig-ftli-region-tikz"/>, the region <m>D</m> is drawn as a
translucent solid and the curve <m>C</m> is in red. According
to the fundamental theorem, the line integral of a conservative vector
field <m>\mathbf F</m> can be easily calculated by calculating the
values of its corresponding potential function <m>f</m>
(<xref ref="def-conservative-concepts"/>) at the beginning
(point <m>A</m>) and end (point <m>B</m>) of the curve <m>C</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ftli-region-tikz">
<caption>
A smooth curve <m>C</m> joining <m>A(x_1,y_1,z_1)</m> to
<m>B(x_2,y_2,z_2)</m> inside an open region <m>D</m> in space, drawn as
a translucent solid containing <m>C</m>. For a conservative field
<m>\mathbf F = \nabla f</m>, the line integral along <m>C</m> equals
<m>f(B) - f(A)</m>, no matter how <m>C</m> winds from <m>A</m> to
<m>B</m>.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<shortdescription>
A curve winding from point A to point B inside a dotted region in space.
</shortdescription>
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space with a translucent blue solid marking an
open region D. Inside the solid, a red curve C winds from the point A
with coordinates x 1 comma y 1 comma z 1 to the point B with
coordinates x 2 comma y 2 comma z 2, with an arrowhead at the middle of
the curve showing the direction of travel from A to B.
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \definecolor{oigreen}{RGB}{0,158,115}
  \definecolor{oipurple}{RGB}{204,121,167}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={115}{20},
      width=12cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=-0.4, xmax=2.7, ymin=-0.6, ymax=3.3, zmin=-0.2, zmax=2.7,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % ---- the region D: a translucent solid (ellipsoid) containing C ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=interp, draw=none,
        colormap={dblue}{color=(oiblue!12!white) color=(oiblue!55!white)},
        opacity=0.30, fill opacity=0.30,
        domain=0:360, samples=61,
        y domain=-90:90, samples y=31,
        z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({1.25 + 1.2*cos(y)*cos(x)},
       {1.35 + 1.75*cos(y)*sin(x)},
       {1.15 + 1.1*sin(y)});
    % parallels and meridians, so D reads as a solid rather than a flat disc
    \foreach \vv in {-55,-25,10,45}{
      \edef\tmp{\noexpand\addplot3[oiblue!55, line width=0.5pt, domain=0:360,
          samples=97, samples y=0, forget plot]
        ({1.25 + 1.2*cos(\vv)*cos(x)}, {1.35 + 1.75*cos(\vv)*sin(x)},
         {1.15 + 1.1*sin(\vv)});}\tmp}
    \foreach \uu in {0,30,...,330}{
      \edef\tmp{\noexpand\addplot3[oiblue!40, line width=0.4pt, domain=-90:90,
          samples=49, samples y=0, forget plot]
        ({1.25 + 1.2*cos(x)*cos(\uu)}, {1.35 + 1.75*cos(x)*sin(\uu)},
         {1.15 + 1.1*sin(x)});}\tmp}
    \node[anchor=south, font=\large] at (axis cs:1.25,1.35,2.32) {$D$};
    % ---- the curve C from A to B (a gentle S-curve, in red/vermillion) ----
    \addplot3[oivermillion, line width=1.7pt,
        domain=0:1, samples=81, samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({0.9 + 0.5*x + 0.45*sin(360*x)},
       {0.15 + 2.45*x},
       {0.6 + 1.1*x});
    % direction arrowhead placed near the middle of C
    \addplot3[oivermillion, line width=1.7pt, -{Latex[length=2.6mm]},
        domain=0.40:0.50, samples=21, samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({0.9 + 0.5*x + 0.45*sin(360*x)},
       {0.15 + 2.45*x},
       {0.6 + 1.1*x});
    \node[oivermillion, anchor=south east, font=\large]
      at (axis cs:1.05,1.5,1.45) {$C$};
    % ---- endpoints ----
    \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.8pt, color=oiblue,
        forget plot] coordinates {(0.9,0.15,0.6) (1.4,2.6,1.7)};
    \node[anchor=south, xshift=-8pt, font=\small] at (axis cs:0.9,0.15,0.68)
      {$A(x_1,y_1,z_1)$};
    \node[anchor=south west, font=\small] at (axis cs:1.42,2.62,1.78)
      {$B(x_2,y_2,z_2)$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The central idea of the theorem<mdash/>that only the endpoints
matter<mdash/>is animated in <xref ref="fig-ftli-video"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ftli-video">
<caption>
Animation of the fundamental theorem of line integrals for the field
<m>\mathbf F = \nabla f</m> with
<m>f(x,y,z) = 1/\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}</m>. Two different curves join
<m>(1,0,0)</m> to <m>(1,0,2\pi)</m>: the helix <m>C_1</m> and the
straight segment <m>C_2</m>. The accumulated value of each line
integral is tracked as the paths are traced, and both integrals arrive
at the same value <m>f(B) - f(A)</m>.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-ftli-path-independence"
       source="videos/ftli_path_independence_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<p>
In the next example, we will see how we can use
<xref ref="thm-ftli"/>.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-ftli-helix">
<title>Example I</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the scalar-valued function
<m>f(x,y,z) = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}}</m>.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Calculate the gradient vector field
<m>\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \nabla f</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Evaluate the line integral of the vector field
<m>\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \nabla f</m> over the curve <m>C_1</m>
parametrized as
<m>\mathbf r(t) = \cos(t)\mathbf i + \sin(t)\mathbf j + t\mathbf k</m>
from <m>(1,0,0)</m> to <m>(1,0,2\pi)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
What is the result of <m>\int_{C_2} \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r</m> from
<m>(1,0,0)</m> to <m>(1,0,2\pi)</m> where <m>C_2</m> is the line
connecting the two points?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,13.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the three parts of Example I.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-helix-segment-tikz">
<caption>
The two paths of <xref ref="example-ftli-helix"/> joining
<m>(1,0,0)</m> to <m>(1,0,2\pi)</m>: the helix <m>C_1</m>, parametrized
by <m>\mathbf r(t) = \cos(t)\mathbf i + \sin(t)\mathbf j + t\mathbf k</m>,
and the straight segment <m>C_2</m>.
</caption>
<image width="75%">
<shortdescription>
A helix and a vertical segment joining the same two points in space.
</shortdescription>
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space showing a blue helix that winds once
around the z axis, climbing from the point one comma zero comma zero up
to the point one comma zero comma two pi, and a vertical vermillion
segment joining the same two points directly. Both endpoints are
marked and labeled.
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \definecolor{oigreen}{RGB}{0,158,115}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={115}{16},
      width=12.5cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
