</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-potential-function">
<title>Finding a Potential Function</title>

<example xml:id="example-potential-2d">
<title>Example II</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the vector field
<m>\mathbf F = (2xy + 3)\mathbf i + (x^2 - 4y)\mathbf j</m> in the
plane.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find a function <m>f</m> such that <m>\mathbf F = \nabla f</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Use the solution of part (A) to calculate the line integral of
<m>\mathbf F</m> along any curve from <m>(0,0)</m> to <m>(1,2)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,15);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,14.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the two parts of Example II.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-potential-function">
<title>Example III</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the vector field
<m>\mathbf F = yz\,\mathbf i + xz\,\mathbf j + (xy + 2z)\mathbf k</m>.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find a function <m>f</m> such that <m>\mathbf F = \nabla f</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Use the solution of part (A) to calculate the line integral from
<m>(1,0,-2)</m> to <m>(4,6,3)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,17);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,16.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the two parts of Example III.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<!-- Example 1.4.10 revisited: the same work integral, but now with a    -->
<!-- potential function and the Fundamental Theorem instead of a         -->
<!-- parametrization.                                                    -->
<example xml:id="example-gravity-ftli">
<title>Example IV</title>
<statement>
<p>
In <xref ref="ex-helix-gravity"/> we computed the work done by the
inverse-square field
</p>

<me>
\mathbf F = \frac{\langle -x,\,-y,\,-z\rangle}
                 {\left(x^2+y^2+z^2\right)^{3/2}}
          = -\frac{\mathbf r}{\lVert\mathbf r\rVert^{3}}
</me>

<p>
along the helix <m>\mathbf r(t) = \langle \cos t,\ \sin t,\ t\rangle</m>
from <m>(1,0,0)</m> to <m>(1,0,2\pi)</m>, by parametrizing the curve.
Do it again with <xref ref="thm-ftli"/>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,13.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for computing the work of Example IV with the fundamental theorem.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-conservative-gradient">
<title>Conservative Fields are Gradient Fields</title>

<theorem xml:id="thm-conservative-gradient">
<title>Conservative Fields are Gradient Fields</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>\mathbf F = M\mathbf i + N\mathbf j + P\mathbf k</m> be a vector
field whose components are continuous throughout an open connected
region <m>D</m> in space (<xref ref="def-conservative-concepts"/>).
Then <m>\mathbf F</m> is conservative if and
only if <m>\mathbf F</m> is a gradient field <m>\nabla f</m> for a
differentiable function <m>f</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,18);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,17.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the proof that conservative fields are gradient fields.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</proof>
</theorem>

<p>
Path independence means that all of the paths in
<xref ref="fig-path-independence-prefigure"/> give the same value of the
line integral: if <m>\mathbf F</m> is conservative, then
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-three-paths">
\int_{C_1} \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
= \int_{C_2} \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
= \int_{C_3} \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
= f(B) - f(A).
</men>

<figure xml:id="fig-path-independence-prefigure">
<caption>
Three different paths <m>C_1</m>, <m>C_2</m>, and <m>C_3</m> joining
<m>A</m> to <m>B</m>. For a conservative field, all three line
integrals have the same value <m>f(B) - f(A)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<shortdescription>
Three different curves joining the points A and B.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-path-independence">
<diagram dimensions="(340,280)" margins="10">
<definition>c1(t) = (4*t - 1.6*t*(1-t), 3*t - 3.4*t*(1-t))</definition>
<definition>c2(t) = (4*t, 3*t)</definition>
<definition>c3(t) = (4*t - 3.4*t*(1-t), 3*t + 3.2*t*(1-t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.3,-1.2,5.3,4.6)">
<parametric-curve at="pi-c1" function="c1" domain="(0,1)"
    stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<parametric-curve at="pi-c2" function="c2" domain="(0,1)"
    stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<parametric-curve at="pi-c3" function="c3" domain="(0,1)"
    stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<!-- The direction of travel is shown at the midpoint of each path,     -->
<!-- rather than at its end, where the three arrowheads crowded the     -->
<!-- point B.  Tail = midpoint - v/2, so each arrow straddles the       -->
<!-- middle of its curve, pointing along the tangent there.             -->
<vector at="pi-c1-arrow" v="(0.72,0.54)" tail="(1.24,0.38)"
    stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="pi-c2-arrow" v="(0.72,0.54)" tail="(1.64,1.23)"
    stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="pi-c3-arrow" v="(0.72,0.54)" tail="(0.79,2.03)"
    stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<point at="pi-A" p="(0,0)" fill="black" alignment="sw">
<m>A</m>
</point>
<point at="pi-B" p="(4,3)" fill="black" alignment="ne">
<m>B</m>
</point>
<label at="pi-c1-label" anchor="(2.95,1.25)" alignment="se"
    color="#0072B2"><m>C_1</m></label>
<label at="pi-c2-label" anchor="(3.15,2.15)" alignment="se"
    color="#009E73"><m>C_2</m></label>
<label at="pi-c3-label" anchor="(2.35,3.0)" alignment="nw"
    color="#D55E00"><m>C_3</m></label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Two points A and B in the plane joined by three different curves. C 1 bows below the straight path, C 2 is the straight segment from A to B, and C 3 bows above. An arrow at the middle of each curve points from A to B.">
  <annotation ref="pi-A" text="The starting point A of all three paths."/>
  <annotation ref="pi-B" text="The ending point B of all three paths."/>
  <annotation ref="pi-c1" text="The path C 1, drawn in blue, bowing below the straight segment, with an arrow at its midpoint pointing from A toward B."/>
  <annotation ref="pi-c2" text="The path C 2, the straight segment from A to B, drawn in green, with an arrow at its midpoint."/>
  <annotation ref="pi-c3" text="The path C 3, drawn in vermillion, bowing above the straight segment, with an arrow at its midpoint."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-loop-property">
<title>The Loop Property of Conservative Fields</title>

<theorem xml:id="thm-loop-property">
<title>Loop Property of Conservative Fields</title>
<statement>
<p>
The following statements are equivalent.
</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>
<m>\oint_C \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r = 0</m> around every loop (that
is, closed curve <m>C</m>) in <m>D</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The field <m>\mathbf F</m> is conservative on <m>D</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,11.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the proof of the loop property.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</proof>
</theorem>

<figure xml:id="fig-loop-split-prefigure">
<caption>
On the left, a loop <m>C</m> is split into two pieces:
<m>C_1</m> from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m> and <m>C_2</m> from <m>B</m> back
to <m>A</m>, so <m>C = C_1 \cup C_2</m>. On the right, the orientation
of <m>C_2</m> is reversed to produce <m>-C_2</m>; now <m>C_1</m> and
<m>-C_2</m> both connect <m>A</m> to <m>B</m>, and path independence
forces the two integrals to agree.
</caption>
<sidebyside widths="48% 48%">
<image>
<shortdescription>
An oval loop through A and B split into an upper and a lower half.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-loop-split">
<diagram dimensions="(300,260)" margins="10">
<definition>top(t) = (2*cos(t), 1.35*sin(t) + 0.12*sin(2*t))</definition>
<definition>bot(t) = (2*cos(t), 1.35*sin(t) - 0.1*sin(2*t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-3.1,-2.4,3.1,2.4)">
<parametric-curve at="ls-c1" function="top" domain="(0,pi)"
    stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="ls-c2" function="bot" domain="(pi,2*pi)"
    stroke="#CC79A7" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<point at="ls-A" p="(2,0)" fill="black" alignment="e">
<m>A</m>
</point>
<point at="ls-B" p="(-2,0)" fill="black" alignment="w">
<m>B</m>
</point>
<label at="ls-c1-label" anchor="(-0.9,1.55)" alignment="n"
    color="#0072B2"><m>C_1</m></label>
<label at="ls-c2-label" anchor="(1.75,-1.05)" alignment="se"
    color="#CC79A7"><m>C_2</m></label>
<label at="ls-eq" anchor="(0,-1.85)" alignment="s">
<m>C = C_1 \cup C_2</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="An oval loop through the points A on the right and B on the left. The upper half, labeled C 1, is drawn in blue and runs counterclockwise from A to B. The lower half, labeled C 2, is drawn in reddish purple and continues from B back to A, closing the loop.">
  <annotation ref="ls-c1" text="The piece C 1 of the loop, running from A to B along the top."/>
  <annotation ref="ls-c2" text="The piece C 2 of the loop, running from B back to A along the bottom, so the loop is the union of C 1 and C 2."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
<image>
<shortdescription>
The same oval with the lower half reversed so both halves run from A to B.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-loop-reversed">
<diagram dimensions="(300,260)" margins="10">
<definition>top(t) = (2*cos(t), 1.35*sin(t) + 0.12*sin(2*t))</definition>
<definition>botrev(t) = (2*cos(-t), 1.35*sin(-t) + 0.1*sin(2*t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-3.1,-2.4,3.1,2.4)">
<parametric-curve at="lr-c1" function="top" domain="(0,pi)"
    stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="lr-negc2" function="botrev" domain="(0,pi)"
    stroke="#009E73" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<point at="lr-A" p="(2,0)" fill="black" alignment="e">
<m>A</m>
</point>
<point at="lr-B" p="(-2,0)" fill="black" alignment="w">
<m>B</m>
</point>
<label at="lr-c1-label" anchor="(-0.9,1.55)" alignment="n"
    color="#0072B2"><m>C_1</m></label>
<label at="lr-negc2-label" anchor="(1.75,-1.05)" alignment="se"
    color="#009E73"><m>-C_2</m></label>
<label at="lr-eq" anchor="(0,-1.85)" alignment="s">
<m>\int_{C_1}\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
= \int_{-C_2}\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The same oval through A and B, but now both halves run from A to B. The upper half C 1 is drawn in blue and the lower half, relabeled minus C 2 and drawn in green, has its arrow reversed so that it also runs from A to B. Since both curves connect A to B, path independence makes the two line integrals equal.">
  <annotation ref="lr-c1" text="The piece C 1, still running from A to B along the top."/>
  <annotation ref="lr-negc2" text="The reversed piece minus C 2, drawn in green, now running from A to B along the bottom. C 1 and minus C 2 both connect A to B."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</figure>

<p>
We can now collect everything this section has proved. The content of
<xref ref="thm-conservative-gradient"/>, combined with
<xref ref="thm-ftli"/>, the loop property in
<xref ref="thm-loop-property"/>, and the definition of path independence
in <xref ref="def-conservative-concepts"/>, can be organized into the
following square of equivalent statements. Throughout, <m>\mathbf F</m>
is a continuous field with continuous partial derivatives on an open,
connected, simply connected region <m>D</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-equivalence-square-prefigure">
<caption>
The four statements are equivalent. Each double arrow joins the middle of
one box to the middle of the next, and may be read in either direction.
</caption>
<image width="100%">
<shortdescription>
Four boxed statements at the corners of a square, joined by double arrows.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-equivalence-square">
<diagram dimensions="(580,271)" margins="6">
<coordinates bbox="(0,0,12,5.6)">
<!-- Boxes.  Left column centred on x = 2.5, right on x = 9.0; the top   -->
<!-- row centred on y = 4.45, the bottom row on y = 1.45.  Every arrow   -->
<!-- below runs between two of those centres, so it meets each box at    -->
<!-- the middle of an edge.                                              -->
<rectangle at="eq-box-cons" lower-left="(0.2,3.9)" dimensions="(4.6,1.1)"
    stroke="#0072B2" fill="#dbeaf7" thickness="2"/>
<label at="eq-lbl-cons" anchor="(2.5,4.45)" alignment="c">
<m>\mathbf F \text{ is a conservative field}</m>
</label>

<rectangle at="eq-box-grad" lower-left="(6.4,3.6)" dimensions="(5.2,1.7)"
    stroke="#0072B2" fill="#dbeaf7" thickness="2"/>
<label at="eq-lbl-grad1" anchor="(9.0,4.82)" alignment="c">
<m>\mathbf F \text{ is a gradient field}</m>
</label>
<label at="eq-lbl-grad2" anchor="(9.0,4.08)" alignment="c">
<m>\mathbf F = \nabla f \text{ for some } f</m>
</label>

<rectangle at="eq-box-loop" lower-left="(0.2,0.6)" dimensions="(4.6,1.7)"
    stroke="#0072B2" fill="#dbeaf7" thickness="2"/>
<label at="eq-lbl-loop1" anchor="(2.5,1.82)" alignment="c">
<m>\oint_C \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r = 0</m>
</label>
<label at="eq-lbl-loop2" anchor="(2.5,1.08)" alignment="c">
<m>\text{on every loop}</m>
</label>

<rectangle at="eq-box-path" lower-left="(6.4,0.6)" dimensions="(5.2,1.7)"
    stroke="#0072B2" fill="#dbeaf7" thickness="2"/>
<label at="eq-lbl-path1" anchor="(9.0,1.82)" alignment="c">
<m>\int_C \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r \text{ is path independent}</m>
</label>
<label at="eq-lbl-path2" anchor="(9.0,1.08)" alignment="c">
<m>\int_C \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r = f(B) - f(A)</m>
</label>

<!-- Double arrows, centre to centre. -->
<line at="eq-arrow-top" endpoints="((4.8,4.45),(6.4,4.45))"
    stroke="#333333" thickness="2" arrows="2"/>
<line at="eq-arrow-bottom" endpoints="((4.8,1.45),(6.4,1.45))"
    stroke="#333333" thickness="2" arrows="2"/>
<line at="eq-arrow-left" endpoints="((2.5,3.9),(2.5,2.3))"
    stroke="#333333" thickness="2" arrows="2"/>
<line at="eq-arrow-right" endpoints="((9.0,3.6),(9.0,2.3))"
    stroke="#333333" thickness="2" arrows="2"/>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Four boxed statements at the corners of a square, each joined to its neighbours by a double-headed arrow running from the middle of one box to the middle of the next. Top left: F is a conservative field. Top right: F is a gradient field, that is F equals grad f for some f. Bottom left: the loop integral of F dot d r is zero on every loop. Bottom right: the integral of F dot d r is path independent and equals f of B minus f of A. Because every arrow is double-headed, any one of the four statements implies all of the others.">
  <annotation ref="eq-box-cons" text="Top left box: F is a conservative field."/>
  <annotation ref="eq-box-grad" text="Top right box: F is a gradient field, F equals grad f for some potential function f."/>
  <annotation ref="eq-box-loop" text="Bottom left box: the integral of F dot d r around every loop is zero."/>
  <annotation ref="eq-box-path" text="Bottom right box: the integral of F dot d r is path independent, and equals f of B minus f of A."/>
  <annotation ref="eq-arrow-top" text="A double arrow across the top: conservative if and only if gradient field."/>
  <annotation ref="eq-arrow-bottom" text="A double arrow across the bottom: the loop property if and only if path independence."/>
  <annotation ref="eq-arrow-left" text="A double arrow down the left side: conservative if and only if the loop property."/>
  <annotation ref="eq-arrow-right" text="A double arrow down the right side: gradient field if and only if path independence."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Read across the top: a conservative field is exactly a gradient field,
and the potential function <m>f</m> is what makes that concrete. Read
across the bottom: path independence and the loop property are two ways
of saying the same thing, and the Fundamental Theorem turns either one
into a number, <m>f(B) - f(A)</m>. Read down either side: each statement
on the top implies, and is implied by, the one beneath it. In practice
the four are used in whichever direction is convenient<mdash/>find a
potential function to evaluate a line integral, or observe that a field
is a gradient field to conclude at once that its integral around every
loop vanishes.
</p>

</subsection>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<!-- Questions 4-6 of the companion GeoGebra activity, with the three      -->
<!-- applets VF-fig 2, VF-fig 3 and VF-fig 4 (600 x 500).                  -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-conservative-concept-check">
<title>Concept Check</title>

<p>
The three questions below all use the applets that go with them. In each
one, drag the slider to send the point along the curve and watch the field
vector and the tangent vector move with it; the sign of their dot product is
what the integral is accumulating. Every applet can be rotated by dragging
the background and zoomed by scrolling.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="q-vf-helix-work">
<title>Work Along the Helix, Two Ways</title>
<statement>
<p>
For the field and path of <xref ref="fig-vf-helix-work"/>:
</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>
Use the parametrized path to compute the work done by the force field from
point <m>A</m> to point <m>B</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Is it possible to use the fundamental theorem of line integrals
<xref ref="eq-ftli"/> to compute that work? If so, use it to verify your
answer to the previous part. Justify your answer fully.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>
(Read the parametrization and the field off
<xref ref="fig-vf-helix-work"/>, and find the parameter values of
<m>A</m> and <m>B</m>. For part 1, write <m>\mathbf F(\mathbf r(t))</m>
and <m>\mathbf v(t)</m>, dot them, and integrate. For part 2, decide
whether <m>\mathbf F</m> is conservative <mdash/> where is it defined,
and can you find a potential function? <mdash/> and if so evaluate
<m>f(B) - f(A)</m>. Then explain, from the picture, why the two answers
agree.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,15);
\draw[gray!35] (0,12.5) -- (15,12.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7.5) -- (15,7.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,14.7) {$A = \mathbf r(\;\;)$, $B = \mathbf r(\;\;)$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.2) {1. $\mathbf F(\mathbf r(t)) =$ \quad $\mathbf v(t) =$ \quad $\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf v =$ \quad $W =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.2) {2. is $\mathbf F$ conservative? \quad potential $f =$ \quad $W = f(B) - f(A) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {why the two agree, from the picture:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the parameter values of the endpoints, for the direct computation of the work, for the fundamental-theorem computation with its justification, and for the geometric explanation.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-vf-helix-work">
<caption>
A point climbing the helix
<m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\left\langle \cos t,\ \sin t,\ t/2\right\rangle</m> from
<m>A</m> to <m>B</m>, through the force field
<m>\mathbf{F}=\left\langle -x,\ y,\ 0\right\rangle</m>. The two arrows are
<m>\mathbf{F}</m> at the moving point and the velocity <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="arjpdtkt"
             width="100%" aspect="600:500"
             material-width="600" material-height="500"
             shift-drag-zoom="yes" reset-icon="yes">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the slider <m>t</m> to move the point from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m> along
the helix. At each position the applet draws the force
<m>\mathbf{F}=\left\langle -x,y,0\right\rangle</m> acting there and the
velocity <m>\mathbf{v}=d\mathbf{r}/dt</m>. Watch the angle between them: it
is acute on some stretches and obtuse on others, so the work is being added
on some parts of the trip and taken back on others.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<exercise xml:id="q-vf-segment">
<title>A Line Integral Along a Segment</title>
<statement>
<p>
Compute the line integral of the vector field along the line segment shown
in <xref ref="fig-vf-segment"/>.
</p>
<p>
(Read the field and the endpoints off <xref ref="fig-vf-segment"/>.
Before parametrizing anything, test whether the field is conservative,
and look for a potential function by inspection. If you find one, the
segment never needs to be parametrized.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6.5) -- (15,6.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {is $\mathbf F$ conservative?};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.2) {potential: $f(x,y,z) =$ \qquad check $\nabla f =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {$\displaystyle\int_C \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r = f(B) - f(A) =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for testing whether the field is conservative, for a potential function and its check, and for the line integral from the endpoint values.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-vf-segment">
<caption>
The straight segment from <m>A=(0,1,-1)</m> to <m>B=(1,2\sqrt2,2)</m> in the
field <m>\mathbf{F}=\tfrac13\left\langle z,\ z,\ x+y\right\rangle</m>, with
<m>\mathbf{F}</m> and the unit tangent <m>\hat{\mathbf{T}}</m> drawn at the
moving point.
</caption>
<!-- Served from a local copy of the material whose stored window is
     458 + 142 = 600 wide by 500 high, so the narrow panel carrying the
     slider is part of the layout.  (The published material stores a
     1098 + 142 window, which loses the panel when squeezed to 600.) -->
<interactive platform="geogebra" width="100%" aspect="600:500">
<slate surface="geogebra" source="geogebra/vf-fig3-segment.ggb"
       aspect="600:500" material-width="600" material-height="500"/>
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the slider to move the point along the segment from <m>A</m> to
<m>B</m>. The applet draws the field vector <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and the unit
tangent <m>\hat{\mathbf{T}}</m> at that point, so what the integral
accumulates is the length of the projection of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> onto
<m>\hat{\mathbf{T}}</m>. Rotate the view to see how the segment sits
relative to the field.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<exercise xml:id="q-vf-curve">
<title>The Same Endpoints, a Wilder Path</title>
<statement>
<p>
Compute the line integral of the vector field from point <m>A</m> to point
<m>B</m> as shown in <xref ref="fig-vf-curve"/>, where the curve is
parametrized as
</p>

<md>
\mathbf{r}(t)=\left\langle \frac{\ln(t)}{\ln(2)},\ t^{3/2},\
t\cos(\pi t)\right\rangle .
</md>
<p>
(First check the endpoints: evaluate <m>\mathbf r(1)</m> and
<m>\mathbf r(2)</m> and compare with <xref ref="fig-vf-segment"/>. Then
decide whether you need to differentiate <m>t\cos(\pi t)</m> at all, and
say why. Compare your answer with <xref ref="q-vf-segment"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
